 To solve this, we first note that the projection of a vector $\mathbf{v}$ onto another vector $\mathbf{u}$ is given by $\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf{u}} \mathbf{v} = \frac{\mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{v}}{\mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{u}} \mathbf{u}$. Our goal is to find $\mathbf{v}$ given the projections onto two different vectors. Let $\mathbf{v} = (v_1, v_2)$. For the first projection, we have $\operatorname{proj}_{\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}} \mathbf{v} = \frac{2v_1 + v_2}{5} \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{38}{5} \\ \frac{19}{5} \end{pmatrix}$. For the second projection, we have $\operatorname{proj}_{\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}} \mathbf{v} = \frac{2v_1 + 3v_2}{13} \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{58}{13} \\ \frac{87}{13} \end{pmatrix}$. Solving these two equations, we get a system of linear equations. \[
\begin{cases}
\frac{2v_1 + v_2}{5} \cdot 2 = \frac{38}{5} \\
\frac{2v_1 + v_2}{5} = \frac{19}{5} \\
\frac{2v_1 + 3v_2}{13} \cdot 2 = \frac{58}{13} \\
\frac{2v_1 + 3v_2}{13} = \frac{87}{13}
\end{cases}
\] Simplifying, we get \[
\begin{cases}
2v_1 + v_2 = 19 \\
2v_1 + 3v_2 = 29
\end{cases}
\] Subtracting the first equation from the second, we get $2v_2 = 10$, so $v_2 = 5$. Plugging this back into the first equation, we get $2v_1 + 5 = 19$, so $v_1 = 7$. Therefore, $\mathbf{v} = (7, 5)$. </think> <answer> $\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}$ </answer>

